On 2008-12-10, JG Miller <jimmiller@ymail.com> wrote:
> From what I've seen, most variant developers hope at best to get into
> the club, not create enduring improvements to replace nethack and get
> a viable, active game out there for the community again.  

Neither of these was my goal when I created Spork.

I intended (in part) to BBQ a sacred cow and demonstrate that, in fact, many
things which were/are considered "untouchable" by the Dev Team and/or 
community still could produce a playable game after being changed.  The #1 
violator was Elbereth; the #2 was the horrific lack of challenge post-Castle,
combined with the mind-numbing boredom and time-sink of Gehennom.

It has succeeded admirably.

I never was interested in replacing Nethack.  If I had been, I would not
have distributed Spork from the word 'go' as a patch; I would have built
a complete distribution that one could install independently from
vanilla.

> mindset in the community, particularly the developer community, needs
> to change to challenge the dev team.

'Challenge the Dev Team'?  What for, so I can become the next Dev Team?  

I think not.  That option comes with rather a large amount of hassle, 
and I did this in my spare time.  Why would I want to saddle myself 
with project management duties _OUTSIDE_ of work, thus removing even 
more of the spare time I hold precious?

Before you try to lecture about what "needs to change", or what "we should
do", perhaps you should talk to those of who "have done it" first,
rather than attempting to speak for us.

If the player community wants change, they will arrange for change to
happen.  Of course, right now, the community who could most easily do
that (and the ones most interested in doing that) have largely moved on
to Crawl.  Perhaps you are just not looking in the right place for your
"viable, active game".

